wer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364135} {:answer/id #uuid "c87266eb-6f2d-5861-bb16-88682d737679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364136} {:answer/id #uuid "65456865-d282-5ff1-baf8-1c617cf373ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f6eded-d14d-5102-a8cf-e9c780b59fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364135} :answer-field/id #uuid "1888a5e7-c78d-5503-b0b1-14a906b14550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2b1c7be-1664-54ba-8015-a807eb2f1187" :question/math-academy-id "q-38033" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{8}\\to {R}^{8}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(T)<8$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{8}$ into ${R}^{8},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=8 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 8.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{rank}(T)<8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124549 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc785eb1-cb7e-5368-a092-51f87b7f7c35" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Square Root Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050642 :topic/math-academy-id 1875 :topic/title "Properties of Transformed Square Root Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2da212-c68b-59f4-ac34-efed874ec6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123483} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8fda97-87b5-55ec-97c6-03c5a47e42a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123484} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbfc8a2-d30d-5327-ae8e-f164e33fd95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123485} {:answer/id #uuid "f68aa48b-13a5-549b-83cf-f6cc4298e792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123486} {:answer/id #uuid "fde7c21e-86ff-5be8-baec-01ff0de00cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123487}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0811cda8-2137-5c27-92be-102b8f4ac2b2" :answer-field/key 